{
  "body_human_readable": "Some of the most widely used tools for working with ontologies include:\n\n- **Prot\u00e9g\u00e9:** The most comprehensive and widely used ontology editor, supporting a large variety of ontology formats and offering extensive plugin support and integration capabilities.\n- **TopBraid Composer:** An all-encompassing knowledge graph modeling environment that supports multiple data formats and provides both graphical and API access.\n- **OntoStudio:** A multilingual editor and ontology modeler with strong support for OWL, RDF, and ObjectLogic, featuring a collaborative server option and integration with OntoBroker for querying with SPARQL.\n- **Fluent Editor:** A free, open-source tool allowing editing of ontologies in natural language with SWRL rule support and interactive visualizations.\n- **Swoop:** A web-oriented OWL ontology browser and editor with annotation and integration features, along with advanced navigation tools.\n- **VocBench:** Provides a collaborative environment for the development of RDF datasets, ontologies, thesauri, and lexicons, with low installation requirements.\n- **OBO-Edit:** Specially designed for editing OBO format ontologies, featuring an intuitive graph-based editor.\n  \nOther tools mentioned in the ontology engineering landscape include Ontolingua Server, Neon Toolkit, KAON2, WebODE, Chimaera, and SKOSed, supporting various aspects of ontology creation, integration, and reasoning functions.\n\nThese tools offer a range of features such as graphical editors, version control, plugin/extensions, collaborative environments, and support for multiple ontology languages and standards (OWL, RDF, etc.) [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7].\n\nFor an at-a-glance comparison of features (usability, scalability, stability, integration, documentation, originality), see Table 4 in the reference material, which evaluates Prot\u00e9g\u00e9, TopBraid Composer, OntoStudio, Fluent Editor, VocBench, Swoop, and OBO-Edit side by side [8].",
  "request": {
